                <pb facs="0563" n="551" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg551"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00116 European War 1939/42: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bullitt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-09-08T15:00:00+01:00">September 8,
                            1939—3 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 3:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1844. Your circular September 4, 2 p.m. Competent French military authorities
                    have informed our Military Attaché that they are glad to authorize the officers
                    on the staffs of our Military and Naval Attaches at Paris to inspect promptly by
                    personal [apparent omission] the scene of any bombing of a non-military
                    objective in France. (According to the staff officer with whom Military Attaché
                    talked, General Gamelin had endorsed “<hi rendition="#italic">tres bien</hi>” on
                    this order.)</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
